explanation:
This is a helper map, not a standard object from the literature. It only serves to build the standard presentation of ⋀[R]^n M. `isPresentationCore` and `exteriorPower.presentation` use it to turn the universal property of the exterior power into the language of `Module.Relations`.

The map itself is correct. The relations defined in `relations` are:
- `add`: f(update m i x) + f(update m i y) − f(update m i (x+y)) = 0;
- `smul`: f(update m i (r•x)) − r•f(update m i x) = 0;
- `alt`: f(m) = 0 whenever m i = m j for some i ≠ j.

These are exactly the axioms of an alternating multilinear map, and the two directions of the bijection simply keep the same underlying function. Since it is a technical tool for the presentation API and not a mathematical concept in its own right, the verdict is not_a_mathematical_object. There is also no generality problem: it already works for any commutative ring R and any index type ι with decidable equality.
